1
0:00:00,000 --> 0:00:02,700
Have you noticed your
favorite coffee prices lately?

2
0:00:02,700 --> 0:00:06,300
Perhaps the grocery bill
for eggs seems higher this month.

3
0:00:06,300 --> 0:00:09,300
Why do these everyday changes
happen around us?

4
0:00:09,300 --> 0:00:12,300
What forces are at work
behind these shifts?

5
0:00:12,300 --> 0:00:16,000
Today, we unravel economic secrets
behind your daily life.

6
0:00:16,000 --> 0:00:19,300
We will explore microeconomics,
economics on a small scale.

7
0:00:21,300 --> 0:00:23,600
Microeconomics helps us
understand individual choices.

8
0:00:23,600 --> 0:00:26,600
It examines how single people
and businesses decide.

9
0:00:26,600 --> 0:00:30,600
Think of it like looking
at one house in a neighborhood.

10
0:00:30,600 --> 0:00:33,600
We study its unique budget,
resources, and choices.

11
0:00:33,600 --> 0:00:36,000
Every decision affects
that household's well-being.

12
0:00:36,000 --> 0:00:38,700
Microeconomics focuses on these
smaller picture details.

13
0:00:38,700 --> 0:00:42,000
It answers why a company
produces a certain amount.

14
0:00:42,000 --> 0:00:45,000
It explains why you buy
a particular product.

15
0:00:45,000 --> 0:00:47,700
It looks at the specific
supply of goods.

16
0:00:47,700 --> 0:00:50,700
It also considers the specific
demand from buyers.

17
0:00:50,700 --> 0:00:53,000
These individual actions
shape our larger economy.

18
0:00:53,000 --> 0:00:55,300
For example, consider
your weekly grocery run.

19
0:00:55,300 --> 0:00:59,300
You decide which items
to buy and how much to spend.

20
0:00:59,300 --> 0:01:02,600
Your local grocery store
decides which products to stock.

21
0:01:02,600 --> 0:01:05,600
They also set the prices
for their shelves.

22
0:01:05,600 --> 0:01:08,300
Microeconomics explores these
millions of daily choices.

23
0:01:08,300 --> 0:01:10,600
It reveals the patterns
within our marketplace.

24
0:01:10,600 --> 0:01:14,300
Understanding this helps us
see the bigger economic picture.

25
0:01:14,300 --> 0:01:17,300
It's the first step
to understanding global trade.

26
0:01:19,300 --> 0:01:21,300
Resources are never
endless for anyone.

27
0:01:21,300 --> 0:01:24,300
Your time is limited;
your money has boundaries.

28
0:01:24,300 --> 0:01:26,600
This fundamental truth
is called scarcity.

29
0:01:26,600 --> 0:01:29,300
We have unlimited desires,
but limited means.

30
0:01:29,300 --> 0:01:31,600
Scarcity forces us
to make decisions constantly.

31
0:01:31,600 --> 0:01:34,600
We must choose how
to use our available resources.

32
0:01:34,600 --> 0:01:37,600
Consider a household budget
as a clear example.

33
0:01:37,600 --> 0:01:41,000
Your monthly income provides
only so much spending power.

34
0:01:41,000 --> 0:01:44,700
You might want a new
television and a family vacation.

35
0:01:44,700 --> 0:01:47,700
Your budget likely cannot
cover both right now.

36
0:01:47,700 --> 0:01:50,000
You face a choice:
TV or vacation?

37
0:01:50,000 --> 0:01:52,700
This choice illustrates
the core of scarcity.

38
0:01:52,700 --> 0:01:55,000
Businesses face similar
dilemmas every day.

39
0:01:55,000 --> 0:01:58,700
A bakery has a set
amount of flour and sugar.

40
0:01:58,700 --> 0:02:02,700
It must decide how many
cakes versus cookies to bake.

41
0:02:02,700 --> 0:02:05,700
The raw materials are not
infinite for them.

42
0:02:05,700 --> 0:02:08,700
They allocate these ingredients
based on expected sales.

43
0:02:08,700 --> 0:02:11,300
Governments also confront
scarcity in their decisions.

44
0:02:11,300 --> 0:02:14,300
They have limited tax
revenue for public services.

45
0:02:14,300 --> 0:02:17,700
Should funds go to new
schools or better roads?

46
0:02:17,700 --> 0:02:20,300
Every decision means giving
up something else.

47
0:02:20,300 --> 0:02:23,000
These trade-offs are central
to economic life.

48
0:02:23,000 --> 0:02:25,700
Scarcity shapes all individual
and collective actions.

49
0:02:25,700 --> 0:02:28,300
It is the bedrock
of microeconomic thinking.

50
0:02:30,300 --> 0:02:32,600
Supply and demand are
core market forces.

51
0:02:32,600 --> 0:02:34,900
Supply represents how much
sellers offer.

52
0:02:34,900 --> 0:02:37,900
Demand shows how much
buyers want to purchase.

53
0:02:37,900 --> 0:02:41,600
Think about your local
farmers' market on a Saturday.

54
0:02:41,600 --> 0:02:44,300
Many farmers arrive with
fresh, ripe tomatoes.

55
0:02:44,300 --> 0:02:48,000
This total amount of tomatoes
is the market's supply.

56
0:02:48,000 --> 0:02:51,300
Many shoppers also visit,
wanting to buy tomatoes.

57
0:02:51,300 --> 0:02:55,000
Their combined desire for
tomatoes is the market's demand.

58
0:02:55,000 --> 0:02:58,000
If many farmers bring tomatoes,
supply is high.

59
0:02:58,000 --> 0:03:01,000
If few people want tomatoes,
demand is low.

60
0:03:01,000 --> 0:03:04,000
When supply exceeds demand,
prices tend to fall.

61
0:03:04,000 --> 0:03:07,000
Farmers might lower prices
to sell their produce.

62
0:03:07,000 --> 0:03:09,300
Conversely, imagine a
sudden tomato shortage.

63
0:03:09,300 --> 0:03:12,300
Perhaps bad weather damaged
many crops this year.

64
0:03:12,300 --> 0:03:15,300
Now, fewer tomatoes are
available; supply is low.

65
0:03:15,300 --> 0:03:19,000
If many people still want
tomatoes, demand remains high.

66
0:03:19,000 --> 0:03:21,700
When demand exceeds supply,
prices usually rise.

67
0:03:21,700 --> 0:03:25,000
Shoppers might pay more
to get their desired tomatoes.

68
0:03:25,000 --> 0:03:28,000
The price eventually settles
at an equilibrium point.

69
0:03:28,000 --> 0:03:30,700
At this point, supply
matches demand perfectly.

70
0:03:30,700 --> 0:03:33,000
All farmers can sell
their tomatoes.

71
0:03:33,000 --> 0:03:35,300
All interested shoppers
can buy them.

72
0:03:35,300 --> 0:03:38,600
This interplay of supply
and demand shapes all prices.

73
0:03:38,600 --> 0:03:41,900
It affects your coffee,
your eggs, and everything else.

74
0:03:41,900 --> 0:03:44,900
It is an invisible hand
guiding market transactions.

75
0:03:46,900 --> 0:03:49,900
Every decision you make
has a hidden cost.

76
0:03:49,900 --> 0:03:53,600
This cost is the value
of your next best choice.

77
0:03:53,600 --> 0:03:55,900
Economists call this
the opportunity cost.

78
0:03:55,900 --> 0:03:59,600
It is what you give up
by choosing something else.

79
0:03:59,600 --> 0:04:02,900
Imagine you decide to watch
a favorite show tonight.

80
0:04:02,900 --> 0:04:05,600
Your alternative might have
been to exercise.

81
0:04:05,600 --> 0:04:08,900
The health benefits from
exercising are your opportunity cost.

82
0:04:08,900 --> 0:04:11,600
You sacrificed those benefits
for TV entertainment.

83
0:04:11,600 --> 0:04:14,600
It's not about money,
but about lost potential.

84
0:04:14,600 --> 0:04:16,600
Let's consider another
everyday scenario.

85
0:04:16,600 --> 0:04:20,300
You choose to buy a new
pair of stylish shoes.

86
0:04:20,300 --> 0:04:23,300
Perhaps you could have saved
that money instead.

87
0:04:23,300 --> 0:04:26,600
Saving could have contributed
to your future travel fund.

88
0:04:26,600 --> 0:04:29,900
The lost travel fund
contribution is your opportunity cost.

89
0:04:29,900 --> 0:04:32,200
Businesses face these
crucial trade-offs too.

90
0:04:32,200 --> 0:04:35,500
A company decides to invest
in new production machinery.

91
0:04:35,500 --> 0:04:38,800
It must give up investing
in employee training programs.

92
0:04:38,800 --> 0:04:42,500
The potential benefits from
training are their opportunity cost.

93
0:04:42,500 --> 0:04:45,200
Governments weigh opportunity
costs when making policies.

94
0:04:45,200 --> 0:04:49,200
Building a new hospital means
not building a new park.

95
0:04:49,200 --> 0:04:52,200
The community benefits of
the park are sacrificed.

96
0:04:52,200 --> 0:04:54,500
Understanding opportunity cost
improves your decisions.

97
0:04:54,500 --> 0:04:58,200
It helps you recognize the
full implications of choices.

98
0:04:58,200 --> 0:05:01,500
This concept applies to every
aspect of your life.

99
0:05:01,500 --> 0:05:04,800
It shows that "free" choices
rarely exist in reality.

100
0:05:06,800 --> 0:05:10,100
How much does a price
change affect your buying?

101
0:05:10,100 --> 0:05:12,400
Elasticity measures this
responsiveness in economics.

102
0:05:12,400 --> 0:05:15,400
Some products are very
sensitive to price shifts.

103
0:05:15,400 --> 0:05:18,400
Their demand is called "elastic,"
like vacation travel.

104
0:05:18,400 --> 0:05:21,100
If airfares increase, many
people postpone trips.

105
0:05:21,100 --> 0:05:24,100
They seek cheaper alternatives
or stay home instead.

106
0:05:24,100 --> 0:05:27,100
Other products are less
sensitive to price changes.

107
0:05:27,100 --> 0:05:30,100
Their demand is called "inelastic,"
like basic food.

108
0:05:30,100 --> 0:05:33,400
You still buy groceries even
if prices rise slightly.

109
0:05:33,400 --> 0:05:36,700
People need food to live,
regardless of cost.

110
0:05:36,700 --> 0:05:39,000
Consider gasoline prices
at the pump.

111
0:05:39,000 --> 0:05:42,000
When gas prices climb,
you might drive less.

112
0:05:42,000 --> 0:05:45,000
You might combine errands
or choose public transport.

113
0:05:45,000 --> 0:05:48,700
Your demand for gas is
somewhat elastic in this case.

114
0:05:48,700 --> 0:05:52,000
But you still need some
gas for daily commuting.

115
0:05:52,000 --> 0:05:56,000
There is a limit to how
much you can reduce usage.

116
0:05:56,000 --> 0:05:59,300
A dramatic price increase
might change your car choice.

117
0:05:59,300 --> 0:06:02,300
Companies analyze elasticity
for their pricing strategies.

118
0:06:02,300 --> 0:06:05,300
If demand is elastic,
they might lower prices.

119
0:06:05,300 --> 0:06:09,000
This can attract many more
customers and boost sales.

120
0:06:09,000 --> 0:06:12,000
If demand is inelastic,
they can raise prices.

121
0:06:12,000 --> 0:06:15,300
Customers will likely still
buy, accepting the higher cost.

122
0:06:15,300 --> 0:06:17,600
Understanding elasticity helps
explain market behavior.

123
0:06:17,600 --> 0:06:20,900
It shows us why some
product prices fluctuate more.

124
0:06:20,900 --> 0:06:24,900
It applies to everything from
luxury goods to daily necessities.

125
0:06:26,900 --> 0:06:29,600
You now understand the
basics of microeconomics.

126
0:06:29,600 --> 0:06:32,300
We covered individual choices,
scarcity, and trade-offs.

127
0:06:32,300 --> 0:06:35,300
We explored how supply
and demand set prices.

128
0:06:35,300 --> 0:06:38,300
We also learned about price
sensitivity and elasticity.

129
0:06:38,300 --> 0:06:41,000
These concepts explain
the world around you.

130
0:06:41,000 --> 0:06:44,000
From your coffee price
to global market forces.

131
0:06:44,000 --> 0:06:47,300
This is just Phase 1
of your economic journey.

132
0:06:47,300 --> 0:06:50,300
Join us next time for
Macroeconomics & Global Markets.

133
0:06:50,300 --> 0:06:53,600
Subscribe to Eco World
for economics that make sense.